#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676} :activity/id #uuid "045805f2-d7d9-501d-8e4b-541e2bb65e50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/id #uuid "93e6a7ce-2e63-5dea-bbbb-a00575db5770" :topic/math-academy-id 1852} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" :step/math-academy-id 17469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119619 :question/id #uuid "d5a81b39-421e-55c3-90de-d9f4e6489cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4795"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" :step/math-academy-id 17471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"} :step/id #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" :step/math-academy-id 17477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403766}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" :step/math-academy-id 17478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119671 :question/id #uuid "21a6808a-5b1c-5b1d-9bd4-da605fb6d920" :question/math-academy-id "e-6218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fac58a79-2d3f-5ad3-b1ab-c5ce279c4ebe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" :step/math-academy-id 23264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678}}] :activity/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120046}]}
